msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"mappers-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map operator representations {#map-operator-representations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A **mapper** turns a "
"[`BosonOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator\") into a qubit operator, here a "
"[`SparseObservable`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparseObservable)."
" The operator itself knows nothing about qubits: it stores ladder operators "
"on modes, and a bosonic mode holds arbitrarily many particles. Everything "
"that makes the operator finite (how many occupation levels you keep, and how"
" those levels are laid out on qubits) is decided when you map it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The library mapper "
"[`occupation_encoding()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-library-occupation-"
"encoding#qiskit_bosons.mappers.library.occupation_encoding "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.library.occupation_encoding\") works in the "
"occupation (Fock) basis: level $n$ of a mode is the state with $n$ bosons. "
"It takes the operator and a "
"[`LevelSpec`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-level-"
"spec#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec \"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec\"), "
"which holds the truncation and the encoding. This guide explains what those "
"two choices mean and what each one costs. For an end-to-end route that uses "
"them, see [Simulate Bose-Hubbard dynamics with a choice of encoding](bose-"
"hubbard#bose-hubbard-getting-started); for how the transpiler applies the "
"same choices to a whole circuit, see [Transpile bosonic "
"circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"truncation-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Truncate the Fock space {#truncate-the-fock-space}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A mode that is kept to $d = n_\\text{max} + 1$ levels, where $n_\\text{max}$"
" is its `max_occupation`, no longer obeys the canonical commutation "
"relation. Writing $\\bar{b}$ for the annihilation operator truncated to "
"those levels,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"[\\bar{b}, \\bar{b}^\\dagger] = 1 - d \\, \\lvert d-1 \\rangle\\langle d-1 \\rvert \\, ,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"so the relation holds on every level except the highest <span id=\"id1\" "
"/>[\\[1\\]](#id4). You can see this by mapping the commutator of the two "
"ladder operators and reading its diagonal on the levels of one mode. The "
"following helper builds the dense matrix of a mapped operator for a single "
"mode, which is only reasonable for very small registers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The last entry is $1 - d$. Because this means the span of "
"$\\{\\bar{b}^\\dagger, \\bar{b}\\}$ is not closed under the commutator, no "
"encoding is an isomorphism of algebras. An encoding maps *states*, together "
"with the operators that act within the truncated subspace. The following two"
" consequences matter in practice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The cutoff must bound the reachable occupation.** It is not enough to "
"bound the occupation you start with. A beamsplitter acting on $\\lvert 1, 1 "
"\\rangle$ populates $\\lvert 2, 0 \\rangle$ and $\\lvert 0, 2 \\rangle$, so "
"a cutoff of one silently produces a wrong state:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With a cutoff of one, the probability of staying in $\\lvert 1, 1 \\rangle$ "
"is one, because the two states that the dynamics would populate do not "
"exist. With a cutoff of two, it vanishes, which is the Hong-Ou-Mandel "
"effect. This is why `max_occupation` is a required argument that never has a"
" default: it is a modeling decision and a convergence parameter that you are"
" expected to sweep.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Mapping does not commute with normal ordering.** The operators $b "
"b^\\dagger$ and $b^\\dagger b + 1$ are equal before truncation, but they map"
" to different qubit operators:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two agree on every level but the highest. Applied to the top level, "
"$b^\\dagger$ has nowhere to go and the amplitude is clipped, so $b "
"b^\\dagger$ is zero there, whereas $b^\\dagger b + 1$ gives $d$. Decide once"
" whether your Hamiltonian is defined in normal-ordered form, and map that "
"form consistently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Three methods help you choose a cutoff, and all of them are lower bounds. "
"The operator reports the largest number of creation operators that it "
"applies to each mode within a single term, and a spec compares that with its"
" cutoffs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A non-zero entry means the cutoff is below the bound, and the value is how "
"far. These helpers do not know about the dynamics, so a passing check does "
"not make a cutoff sufficient; the beamsplitter above has a bound of one yet "
"needs a cutoff of two. They can also report a shortfall for a term that is "
"zero on the retained levels, as $b^\\dagger b^\\dagger b b$ is for a cutoff "
"of one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"level-spec-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Describe the truncation with a LevelSpec {#describe-the-truncation-with-a-"
"levelspec}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A [`LevelSpec`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-"
"level-spec#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec\") bundles the cutoff of every mode with "
"the encoding, and it derives from them how many qubits each mode occupies "
"and where its qubits start. Everything that has to agree about that (the "
"mapper, the transpiler layout, the state preparation) is given the same "
"spec. The encoding is a required argument, and so is the cutoff: neither has"
" a default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cutoff is either one integer, shared by all modes, or one integer per "
"mode:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A spec built from a single integer leaves the number of modes open, so you "
"have to resolve it before asking for widths. A sequence is never broadcast, "
"whatever its length, and it fixes the number of modes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Mode $m$ occupies the qubits `offsets[m]` to `offsets[m] + widths[m] - 1`, "
"so modes with different cutoffs can take different numbers of qubits, and "
"the offsets are the running sum of the widths. You do not need to pass the "
"number of modes to the mapper. It takes it from the spec, or, for a spec "
"with a single cutoff, from the operator through "
"[`min_num_modes()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes\"). The register can "
"be wider than the modes need; the surplus qubits are idle and sit at the "
"end:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An operator that acts on a mode beyond the last one of a per-mode spec is "
"refused:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A few limits follow from how the encodings count qubits. Under a compact "
"encoding, a mode with a single level (`max_occupation = 0`) takes no qubit "
"at all, because its one level needs nothing to be told apart from another. "
"Such a mode is allowed and does not move the qubits of the modes after it, "
"but a spec in which every mode would take no qubit is rejected. The unary "
"encodings hold at most 128 levels per mode, and a larger cutoff raises an "
"error that suggests a compact encoding:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choose an encoding {#choose-an-encoding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A [`LevelEncoding`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-"
"level-encoding#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding\") decides which basis state of a "
"block of qubits represents each level. There are four, and no one of them is"
" best in general <span id=\"id2\" />[\\[2\\]](#id5). The number of qubits "
"per mode and the bit pattern of each level follow from the encoding:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The bit patterns are integers in which bit $q$ is the value of qubit $q$. "
"The unary encodings spend one qubit per level and set one of them. The "
"compact encodings spend $\\lceil \\log_2 d \\rceil$ qubits and store the "
"level number, in binary or as a Gray code in which neighboring levels differ"
" in one bit. The string name of an encoding is accepted wherever the "
"encoding is.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The trade-off is qubits against operator weight. The compact encodings need "
"exponentially fewer qubits, but a ladder operator connects levels whose "
"patterns can differ in several bits, so its terms act on more qubits. The "
"following sections look at what this costs, starting with the two unary "
"encodings, which have identical layouts and differ only in the operator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"unary-variants-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compare the two unary encodings {#compare-the-two-unary-encodings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the unary encoding a ladder operator moves one excitation between two "
"neighboring qubits. To write that as an operator on the whole mode, you must"
" choose what to do with the other qubits. The two variants choose "
"differently:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`unary_projected` puts a projector $\\lvert 0 \\rangle\\langle 0 \\rvert$ on"
" every other qubit of the mode. That enforces the one-hot constraint in the "
"operator itself: the term acts only if the rest of the mode is empty.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`unary_unprojected` leaves those qubits alone, so the term acts as the "
"identity on them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Look at a single $b^\\dagger$ at $d = 4$ under both. The mapper returns a "
"[`SparseObservable`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparseObservable),"
" whose terms are strings of single-qubit bit terms. Here are the first four "
"terms of each, with the qubits they act on:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both have twelve terms with the same coefficients. The projected terms carry"
" a `0` bit term on the two qubits that the unprojected terms do not mention,"
" so each projected term touches all four qubits and each unprojected term "
"touches two. In general a projected term touches all $d$ qubits of the mode,"
" because it carries two factors, each $X$ or $Y$, and a projector on each "
"other qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two operators are equal on the states that the encoding is meant for, "
"the one-hot basis states (the codewords), and differ outside them. To check,"
" take a small operator on two modes at $d = 3$ and compare the matrices on "
"the nine codewords:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both also leave the codeword subspace invariant, so a state prepared on a "
"codeword stays on codewords. The choice matters only for states that leave "
"that subspace, for example through noise. There the projected operator "
"suppresses the evolution, because its projectors fail, whereas the "
"unprojected one keeps acting. Neither behavior is a physical requirement. "
"The unprojected operator is only defined on the one-hot subspace, so "
"choosing it is a modeling decision and not a free optimization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What the projectors cost is gates. Evolve a Hermitian $b^\\dagger + b$ on "
"one mode for a short time, and count the two-qubit gates after Qiskit’s "
"transpilation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The exact counts depend on how Qiskit synthesizes the evolution gate, which "
"changes between releases, so plot them:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![CX count against the number of levels d for the four encodings. The "
"unary\\_projected count grows fastest, the unary\\_unprojected count grows "
"linearly, and the Gray code is the cheapest.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-14.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The unprojected count grows linearly with $d$, because each of the $d - 1$ "
"transitions is a two-qubit rotation. The projected count grows much faster, "
"because every term acts on all $d$ qubits. The compact encodings need fewer "
"qubits, but the weight of their terms depends on how the bit patterns of "
"neighboring levels differ: the Gray code, in which they differ in one bit, "
"is the cheaper of the two compact encodings, and standard binary jumps when "
"a carry flips several bits at once ($d = 5$). Whether a compact encoding "
"also beats the unprojected unary one depends on $d$ and on how Qiskit "
"synthesizes the evolution, so read the trend and not the ranking at one "
"size.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The projectors also matter when you expand a mapped operator into Paulis, "
"which "
"[`from_sparse_observable()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparsePauliOp#from_sparse_observable)"
" does by replacing each projector with a sum of two Pauli operators. A term "
"with $k$ projectors becomes $2^k$ Pauli terms. Here is a single $b^\\dagger$"
" at small $d$, which is the only size at which you should try this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both variants store the same number of terms, but the expansion of the "
"projected one grows exponentially in $d$. For anything larger than a toy "
"example, read the terms of the "
"[`SparseObservable`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparseObservable)"
" directly, as the listing above does, instead of converting it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The compact encodings cannot drop their projectors. There the zero bits are "
"part of the level number, so removing a $\\lvert 0 \\rangle\\langle 0 "
"\\rvert$ changes which levels a term connects, and the operator is no longer"
" the same on the codewords. This is why only the unary encoding comes in two"
" variants.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A product of ladders is truncated first {#a-product-of-ladders-is-truncated-"
"first}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One more design choice shows up in the cost of diagonal terms. The mapper "
"does not map each ladder operator and multiply the results. It collects the "
"ladder operators that a term applies to one mode, multiplies them as a $d "
"\\times d$ matrix in the Fock basis, truncates that product once, and "
"encodes the result <span id=\"id3\" />[\\[2\\]](#id5). A product such as the"
" number operator $b^\\dagger b$ therefore stays diagonal:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Its terms are the projectors $\\lvert 1 \\rangle\\langle 1 \\rvert$ on the "
"qubit of each level above zero, weighted by the level number (level zero has"
" weight zero and so has no term). A time evolution of it is a set of single-"
"qubit rotations and needs no two-qubit gate. Multiplying the two mapped "
"ladder operators instead gives the same operator on the codewords, but a "
"different one outside them, with off-diagonal strings that cost gates:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Diagonal terms such as the on-site interaction of the Bose-Hubbard model are"
" these products, which is where this choice matters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Write a custom mapper {#write-a-custom-mapper}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you want an encoding that the library does not provide, build it from the"
" image of a single ladder operator. "
"[`map_boson_action_generators()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-map-boson-action-"
"generators#qiskit_bosons.mappers.map_boson_action_generators "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.map_boson_action_generators\") takes a function that"
" maps one [`BosonAction`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-action-boson-"
"action#qiskit_bosons.operators.boson_action.BosonAction "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.boson_action.BosonAction\"), a function that "
"returns the identity, and builds the mapper of whole operators from them. It"
" multiplies the images of the actions of each term in order, scales by the "
"coefficient, and sums the terms. The output can be any type that supports "
"scalar multiplication and composition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As an example, take the encoding for $d = 2$ that stores a mode in one "
"qubit. A creation operator is $\\lvert 1 \\rangle\\langle 0 \\rvert$, which "
"is $(X - iY)/2$ in Qiskit’s convention, and an annihilation operator is its "
"adjoint:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This hand-written mapper multiplies the mapped ladder operators, and it "
"agrees with the library mapper here because every mode has two levels and a "
"qubit holds the whole mode. At larger $d$, as the previous section showed, "
"composing the mapped ladders and truncating the product first are not the "
"same operator outside the codewords.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To use such a mapper in a circuit, wrap it in a function with the signature "
"`(operator, num_qubits) -> SparseObservable` and hand it to "
"[`MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/transpiler-passes-synthesis-mapper-fn-evolution-"
"synthesis#qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis"
" \"qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis\"), "
"which maps the operator of every "
"[`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/circuit-"
"library-evolution#qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.Evolution "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate with it. The transpiler "
"pipeline is described in [Transpile bosonic "
"circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two details are worth knowing. First, the qubit operator must have real "
"coefficients, because "
"[`PauliEvolutionGate`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate)"
" rejects an imaginary part. A Hermitian operator maps to a real one, but "
"only once the terms that cancel have been merged. This hand-written mapper "
"does not merge them, so a hopping term and its adjoint leave imaginary "
"coefficients behind until you simplify the result. The library mapper merges"
" its own result; a custom one has to do it, for example by wrapping it in "
"[`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/transpiler-"
"passes-synthesis-simplify#qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.simplify"
" \"qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.simplify\"):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second, a product formula synthesizes the terms in the order it receives "
"them, and the order a mapper produces is not guaranteed to be stable. The "
"helpers [`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/transpiler-passes-synthesis-"
"simplify#qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.simplify "
"\"qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.simplify\") and "
"[`group_wise()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/transpiler-"
"passes-synthesis-group-"
"wise#qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.group_wise "
"\"qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.synthesis.group_wise\") wrap a mapper "
"function to control it: `simplify` merges duplicate strings and sorts the "
"terms into a canonical order, whereas `group_wise` maps one group of the "
"operator at a time so that the terms of a group stay adjacent. Do not "
"combine the two, because sorting discards the grouping. The [grouping "
"guide](grouping#grouping-explanation) shows how to assign groups, and the "
"[synthesis guide](bosonic-synthesis#bosonic-synthesis-explanation) shows how"
" the order affects the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

